{"abstract":"Museums are interested in designing emotional visitor experiences to complement traditional interpretations. HCI is interested in the relationship between Affective Computing and Affective Interaction. We describe Sensitive Pictures, an emotional visitor experience co-created with the Munch art museum. Visitors choose emotions, locate associated paintings in the museum, experience an emotional story while viewing them, and self-report their response. A subsequent interview with a portrayal of the artist employs computer vision to estimate emotional responses from facial expressions. Visitors are given a souvenir postcard visualizing their emotional data. A study of 132 members of the public (39 interviewed) illuminates key themes: designing emotional provocations; capturing emotional responses; engaging visitors with their data; a tendency for them to align their views with the system's interpretation; and integrating these elements into emotional trajectories. We consider how Affective Computing can hold up a mirror to our emotions during Affective Interaction.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Benford, Steve","Løvlie, Anders Sundnes","Ryding, Karin","Rajkowska, Paulina","Bodiaj, Edgar","Paris Darzentas, Dimitrios","Cameron, Harriet","Spence, Jocelyn","Egede, Joy","Spanjevic, Bogdan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384801"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Serbia","Sweden","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","NextGame","University of Nottingham","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Affective Computing Affective Interaction Emotion Affect Museum Data Souvenir Interpretation Ambiguity Computer Vision"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sensitive pictures emotional interpretation in the museum","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/93182157/sensitive_pictures_arxiv.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384801","ids":["itu-eff3362f-375b-40f1-8814-0de4343507f7"],"keyword":["Affective Computing, Affective Interaction, Emotion, Affect, Museum, Data Souvenir, Interpretation, Ambiguity, Computer Vision"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-F6ED16962D7777C68AACCAE9890E8E2D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/93182157/sensitive_pictures_arxiv.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of nottingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Nottingham","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Audio-Visual Computing","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"se","key":"uppsala university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Uppsala University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"rs","key":"nextgame","loc":["Serbia","Europe, Non-EU"],"name":"NextGame","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Steve","key":"benfordpau","last":"Benford","name":"Benford, Steve","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Sundnes","key":"løvliepau","last":"Løvlie","locid":"22ec9269-2fff-4dbf-a9c7-5037559a15ee","name":"Løvlie, Anders Sundnes","orcid":"0000-0003-0484-4668","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":